/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  lkp02_n2_ch4_ar_112K -- THE LEE-KESLER EQUATION OF STATE WITH THE PLOECKER
  MIXING RULES, PREDICTING MEASURED CRYOGENIC VAPOUR-LIQUID EQUILIBRIUM.

  THE QUESTION.  A corresponding-states correlation fitted to pure-fluid
  volumetric and caloric data, plus a one-fluid mixing rule with ONE binary
  constant per pair read from a published table (Ploecker, Knapp & Prausnitz
  1978, Table I): how close does it come to the bubble pressures and vapour
  compositions of the nitrogen + argon + methane system at 112.00 K?  The
  three kappa -- N2-CH4 0.977, N2-Ar 0.988, Ar-CH4 0.984 -- are the table's
  own; nothing is fitted here.

  THE EVIDENCE.  Miller, Kidnay & Hiza, AIChE J. 19 (1973) 145-148: Table 1
  (N2 + Ar, 7 runs), Table 2 (N2 + CH4, 11 runs) and Table 3 (the ternary,
  28 rows, ten liquid rows of which are evaluated below), measured at 112.00 K with the
  nitrogen at T_r = 0.887.  Table I of 1978 lists all three pairs and does not
  say which measurements it was regressed on, so this comparison is NOT
  claimed to be out of sample for kappa; it is a check that the table's
  numbers, used as printed, reproduce measured behaviour.

  WHAT IS COMPARED.  Each liquid composition the experimenter set is evaluated
  at 112 K: P_bubble(x) and the incipient vapour y_eqT(x) -- the engine asked
  where the experimenter put the liquid, never a value read off a curve.  The
  residual is model - measurement, in the aad rows of `expected`.  In the
  binary tables the y of the vapour-sample row stands beside the x of the
  liquid-sample row; the two samples were taken at pressures that differ by up
  to 1.6 %, said in the dataset files, and the dilute end of y is sensitive to
  that: the bubble PRESSURE is the comparison to read, the y a second one.

  WHAT WAS FOUND (the aad rows, and the anchor rows for the ternary).  Bubble
  pressure: N2 + CH4 1.6 % mean absolute, N2 + Ar 0.75 %, the ten ternary
  liquids 0.48 % (largest 1.3 %).  Vapour N2 mole fraction: 0.005 absolute for
  N2 + CH4, 0.013 for N2 + Ar.  Ternary row 10 (x_Ar 0.0878) misses y_N2 by
  0.034; the paper's own activity coefficient for that row, gamma_Ar = 0.661,
  is the odd one out of the table (its neighbours sit between 0.98 and 1.22).

  WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED.  The vapour pressures of the pure components come from
  the catalogue's own records, so part of every residual is theirs.  The
  correlation is for non-polar and slightly polar fluids; these are the
  simplest it covers.  IN SAMPLE: the simple-fluid constants of the
  correlation were fitted to argon, krypton and methane data (Lee & Kesler
  1975, p. 512), so the pure-fluid side of two of these three components is
  not out of sample; the data the paper marks as used in the correlation (its
  Tables 3 and 4) are pure fluids.  Table 3's other
  eighteen rows are not evaluated here.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        pxy_n2_ch4;
        type        propertyScan1D;
        vary        { variable x[N2];
                      points ( 0.0338 0.0545 0.1336 0.1505 0.2170 0.3082 0.4701 0.5172 0.6103 0.7068 0.7811 ); }
        state       { T 112 K;  P 5 atm;  composition { N2 0.5;  CH4 0.5; } }
        properties  ( P_bubble  y_eqT_N2 );
        output      { file pxy_n2_ch4.csv; }
    }
    {
        name        pxy_n2_ar;
        type        propertyScan1D;
        vary        { variable x[N2];
                      points ( 0.1107 0.2452 0.3385 0.5124 0.6243 0.7355 0.8529 ); }
        state       { T 112 K;  P 10 atm;  composition { N2 0.5;  Ar 0.5; } }
        properties  ( P_bubble  y_eqT_N2 );
        output      { file pxy_n2_ar.csv; }
    }
    {
